<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew the Apostle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 60</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saint Andrew</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew the Apostle was one of the twelve apostles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, the brother of Simon Peter and a fisherman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>. Tradition holds that he preached around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and was crucified at Patras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He is the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where by legend his relics were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, and his saltire cross appears on the Scottish and British flags.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle.jpg" alt="Andrew the Apostle" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubens_apostel_andreas_grt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
